---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Navigation from '../../components/Navigation.astro';

const baseUrl = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
// Ensure baseUrl ends with a slash for proper URL construction
const base = baseUrl.endsWith('/') ? baseUrl : `${baseUrl}/`;
---

<Layout>
  <Navigation />
  <div class="container">
    <header class="page-header">
      <a href={`${base}ai-engineering`} class="back-link">
        <svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2">
          <path d="M19 12H5M12 19l-7-7 7-7"/>
        </svg>
        Back to AI Engineering
      </a>
      <h1>Prompt Engineering</h1>
      <p class="subtitle">Techniques and examples for crafting effective AI prompts</p>
    </header>

    <article class="content">
      <section class="intro">
        <p>Prompt engineering is the art and science of crafting inputs that guide AI models to produce high-quality, relevant outputs. Well-designed prompts can transform a basic AI interaction into a powerful tool for analysis, research, and problem-solving.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="example-section">
        <h2>Example: Elite Market Research Prompt</h2>
        <p>Here's a comprehensive prompt template for conducting thorough market research using AI. This example demonstrates how structured, detailed prompts can yield professional-grade analysis.</p>

        <div class="prompt-container">
          <div class="prompt-header">
            <span class="prompt-label">Market Research Prompt Template</span>
          </div>
          <pre class="prompt-content"><code>I'm building [product] for [audience] to solve [problem].

Please analyze:

MARKET SIZE AND GROWTH

• Assess current market size and potential for growth
• Evaluate key trends indicating increasing or declining demand
• Provide evidence from search volumes, surveys, industry data
• Include supporting evidence from Reddit, Amazon, and forums

COMPETITIVE LANDSCAPE

• Identify primary competitors with strengths and weaknesses
• Highlight clear opportunities to differentiate meaningfully
• Assess how sustainable my competitive advantage will be
• Show what makes my solution truly different and better

PRICING AND MARGINS

• Suggest realistic pricing strategies and benchmarks
• Analyze customer willingness to pay based on real data
• Evaluate potential for recurring or expanded revenue
• Find opportunities for complimentary products or upsells

OPERATIONAL FEASIBILITY

• Outline key resources needed to launch successfully
• Evaluate unique operational challenges for this business
• Identify opportunities or limitations in scaling efficiently
• Assess talent, technology, and supplier requirements

MARKETING STRATEGY

• Identify most effective channels for my target market
• Evaluate typical customer acquisition costs vs. lifetime value
• Highlight tactics that will resonate with this audience
• Provide specific marketing approaches with proof they work

RISKS AND REGULATIONS

• Outline significant market, operational, and regulatory risks
• Identify potential barriers to entry or execution
• Assess competitive threats and potential market shifts
• Highlight any intellectual property or legal concerns

LONG-TERM POTENTIAL

• Analyze whether this idea has longevity beyond trends
• Suggest realistic growth paths and potential expansions
• Identify possible pivot options if needed
• Evaluate long-term competitive landscape

VALIDATION APPROACH

• Recommend concrete next steps for testing this idea
• Identify early warning signs that would indicate problems
• Suggest minimum viable test approach before full investment
• Outline key metrics that would indicate success

Provide a clear conclusion on overall viability with specific next steps.</code></pre>
        </div>

        <div class="prompt-analysis">
          <h3>Why This Prompt Works</h3>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Clear Structure:</strong> Organized sections guide the AI to cover all critical aspects of market research</li>
            <li><strong>Specific Instructions:</strong> Each bullet point asks for concrete, actionable information rather than vague generalities</li>
            <li><strong>Evidence-Based:</strong> Requests for data sources (Reddit, Amazon, forums) ensure grounded analysis</li>
            <li><strong>Comprehensive Scope:</strong> Covers market, competition, operations, marketing, risks, and validation</li>
            <li><strong>Actionable Output:</strong> Ends with a request for clear conclusions and next steps</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="example-section">
        <h2>Example: App Idea Prompt – ScreenSeek</h2>
        <p>This prompt describes a complete concept and design spec for an AI-powered screenshot organizer app called ScreenSeek.</p>

        <div class="prompt-container">
          <div class="prompt-header">
            <span class="prompt-label">ScreenSeek App Idea Prompt</span>
          </div>
          <pre class="prompt-content"><code># ScreenSeek Prompt

Create a mobile-first, dark-mode React application called "ScreenSeek" using Tailwind CSS and Lucide React icons. The app is an AI-powered screenshot organizer.

### 1. Design System &amp; Theme

- Background: Deep Slate (bg-slate-900, hex #0f172a).
- Primary Accent: Teal (text-teal-400, bg-teal-500).
- Secondary Accent: Violet (for AI elements).
- Cards/Surfaces: Semi-transparent Slate (bg-slate-800/50) with thin borders (border-slate-700).
- Typography: Sans-serif, clean, white text for headings, slate-400 for secondary text.
- Style: Use rounded corners (rounded-xl or rounded-2xl), subtle glassmorphism (backdrop-blur), and mobile-app styling (fixed bottom navigation).

### 2. Navigation

Create a fixed bottom navigation bar (h-16) with a blur effect (bg-slate-900/90). It should have 4 tabs with icons (use Lucide):

1. Home (LayoutDashboard)
2. Ask AI (MessageSquareText) - Make this the active focus color.
3. Library (Library)
4. Settings (Settings)

### 3. Views &amp; Functionality

A. Dashboard (Home View)

- Header: "Hello, Alex". Subtext: "You have 4 screenshots analyzed."
- Stats Row: Two side-by-side cards:
  1. "Actions" (Icon: Zap): Value "12", Label "Smart actions found".
  2. "Saved" (Icon: TrendingUp): Value "2.5h", Label "Time saved".
- Recent List: A list of recent screenshots with a small thumbnail on the left, title, and category badge.

B. Library View

- Header: "Library" with a count badge.
- Search: A search bar at the top ("Search text in images...").
- Filter Chips: A horizontal scrollable list of pills: All, Travel, Receipts, WiFi, Event.
- Grid: A 2-column grid of Screenshot Cards.
  - Card Design: Aspect ratio 3/4. Image background. Gradient overlay at the bottom.
  - Overlay Content: Category badge (pill shape) and a short text summary.

C. Chat View (Ask AI)

- Interface: A chat interface similar to ChatGPT or iMessage.
- Messages:
  - AI: Slate gray background (bg-slate-800), left-aligned.
  - User: Teal background (bg-teal-600), right-aligned.
- Smart Actions (Crucial Feature): Inside the AI response bubbles, render interactive buttons called "Smart Actions".
  - Design: Dark button with an icon on the left (Calendar, Copy, Map) and text on the right.
  - Behavior: Clicking "Copy" copies text. Clicking "Calendar" shows a simulated alert.
- Input: A text input field fixed at the bottom with a Send button.
- Suggestions: Specific chips above the input: "Where is that flight to Chicago?", "What's the wifi password?".

### 4. Data &amp; Content (Mock Data)

Pre-populate the app with this specific dummy data:

1. United Airlines Flight:
   - Image: https://iili.io/fcBuh0P.png
   - Category: Travel
   - Summary: Flight UA455 to Chicago, Nov 14.
   - AI Logic: If user asks "Where is that flight?", AI responds with flight details and a Smart Action Button labeled "Add to Calendar" (Nov 14, 2:00 PM).

2. Blue Bottle Receipt:
   - Image: https://i.ibb.co/gbLV8qyY/Gemini-Generated-Image-5vq2st5vq2st5vq2.png
   - Category: Receipts
   - Summary: Blue Bottle Coffee receipt for $9.50.

3. WiFi Password:
   - Image: https://i.ibb.co/4xGPNMp/Gemini-Generated-Image-khepsxkhepsxkhep.png
   - Category: WiFi
   - Summary: WiFi Password: Triangle44!
   - AI Logic: If user asks "wifi password", AI responds with the code and a Smart Action Button labeled "Copy Password".

### 5. Technical Requirements

- Use React functional components.
- Use useState for view navigation.
- Do not implement real backend auth, just use local state.
- Ensure the app looks good on mobile dimensions.</code></pre>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="example-section">
        <h2>Example: App Idea Prompt – HospitalityHelp</h2>
        <p>This prompt describes a complete concept and design spec for a staffing insurance platform called HospitalityHelp where businesses pay retainer fees to reserve backup workers for high-risk shifts.</p>

        <div class="prompt-container">
          <div class="prompt-header">
            <span class="prompt-label">HospitalityHelp App Idea Prompt</span>
          </div>
          <pre class="prompt-content"><code># HospitalityHelp Prompt

Build a React application called "**HospitalityHelp**", a staffing insurance platform where businesses pay retainer fees to reserve backup workers for high-risk shifts.

**Tech Stack:**

- React
- Tailwind CSS (Use a Slate/Indigo color palette)
- Lucide React (for icons)
- Recharts (for data visualization)
- Google Gemini API (via @google/genai) for risk analysis.

**Core Functionality:**

The app should have no authentication. Instead, use a "Demo Mode" state to switch between three views: **Landing Page**, **Business Dashboard**, and **Worker Dashboard**.

**1. Landing Page:**

- A high-converting hero section with the headline "Never Let a Shift Go Uncovered".
- Two large CTA buttons: "Business Demo" and "Worker Demo" that switch the main view.
- A feature grid highlighting: "Retainer Payments", "The Panic Button", and "Reputation Scoring".

**2. Business Dashboard (When "Business Demo" is clicked):**

- **Header Stats:** 4 cards showing Active Retainers, Total Savings (green), Reliability Rate, and Active Incidents (red).
- **Create Retainer Form:** Inputs for Role, Date, and Risk Context.
    - Include an "Analyze Risk" button. This should call the Google Gemini API (model: gemini-3-flash-preview) to analyze the description and return a JSON object with: riskLevel (Low/Medium/High/Critical), suggestedRetainer ($), and a reasoning string.
    - Display the AI result in a card before the user allows "Post Retainer".
- **Shift List:** A list of active shifts.
    - If status is "Reserved", show a large red "PANIC BUTTON". Clicking this changes status to "Activated" (simulating a call-out).
    - If status is "Activated", show a "Deploying..." spinner.
- **Analytics:** A simple Bar Chart (using Recharts) showing "Revenue Saved" over 6 months.

**3. Worker Dashboard (When "Worker Demo" is clicked):**

- **Profile Header:** Show a mock user (e.g., "Sarah Jenkins") with a "98 Reputation" score, "Elite" tier, and total earnings.
- **Tabs:** Toggle between "Available Retainers" and "My Standby Schedule".
- **Shift Cards:**
    - Show Role, Business Name, Date/Time, and Risk Level (colored badge).
    - Show the Retainer Fee (e.g., $150) and Surge Rate (e.g., $65/hr).
    - **Action:** If in "Available", button is "Claim Retainer". If in "My Schedule" and status is "Activated", button changes to red "I'm On My Way".

**Design Aesthetics:**

- Use a clean, professional "Inter" font style.
- Use bg-slate-50 for the background and white cards with soft shadows (shadow-sm).
- Use Indigo (indigo-600) for primary actions and Slate (slate-900) for headers.
- Use Red (red-600) specifically for High Risk items and the Panic Button.

**Data:**

- Initialize with dummy data (3-4 existing shifts) so the dashboards aren't empty on load.</code></pre>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="example-section">
        <h2>Example: App Idea Prompt – CalChat</h2>
        <p>This prompt describes a complete concept and design spec for a voice-activated calorie and macro tracking web application called CalChat.</p>

        <div class="prompt-container">
          <div class="prompt-header">
            <span class="prompt-label">CalChat App Idea Prompt</span>
          </div>
          <pre class="prompt-content"><code>Build a voice-activated calorie and macro tracking web application called "CalChat" with the following specifications:

## **Design Requirements**

**Visual Style:**

- Black background with white text throughout the entire app
- Minimalist, clean design with a modern aesthetic
- Use white borders for all UI elements
- High contrast, monochromatic color scheme
- Responsive design that works on mobile and desktop

**Layout:**

- Single page application with centered content
- Maximum width of 4xl for main content area
- 8px padding around the entire page

## **Core Features**

### **1. Header Section**

- Left side: Logo made from a filled MessageSquare icon (size 40) with a small black dot in the center, next to the text "CalChat" in large bold font (text-4xl)
- Right side: Hamburger menu icon (Menu icon, size 24) button that resets all today's entries when clicked
- Menu button should have a subtle border that becomes white on hover

### **2. Macro Display Dashboard**

Display four large cards in a 2x2 grid showing:

- **Calories** (integer, no decimal)
- **Protein** (in grams, 1 decimal place)
- **Carbs** (in grams, 1 decimal place)
- **Fat** (in grams, 1 decimal place)

Each card should have:

- 2px white border
- Large bold numbers (text-5xl)
- Small uppercase label below in gray-400

### **3. Voice Input Component**

- Large circular button (128x128 pixels)
- Center of the page
- White border (2px) when inactive
- Microphone icon when ready to listen
- When active: white background, black icon, pulsing animation
- Text below: "TAP TO SPEAK" (gray, uppercase, small) or "Processing..." when busy
- Status messages appear below showing what was spoken
- Must handle microphone permissions and show clear error messages if denied
- Show browser compatibility message if speech recognition not supported (works in Chrome, Edge, Safari)

### **4. Food Entry List**

- Section titled "TODAY'S ENTRIES" in large bold text
- List of all food entries from today
- Each entry shows:
    - Food name (bold, large)
    - Calories, Protein, Carbs, Fat in a horizontal row
    - Delete button (trash icon) on the right
- Empty state message: "No entries yet. Start by speaking what you've eaten."
- Entries have subtle gray borders that turn white on hover

## **Technical Implementation**

### **Database (Bolt Database)**

Create a `food_entries` table with:

- `id` (uuid, primary key, auto-generated)
- `user_id` (uuid, auto-generated for now)
- `food_name` (text, required)
- `calories` (integer, default 0)
- `protein` (decimal 10,2, default 0)
- `carbs` (decimal 10,2, default 0)
- `fat` (decimal 10,2, default 0)
- `created_at` (timestamptz, auto-set to now)

Enable Row Level Security with public access policies for SELECT, INSERT, and DELETE operations.

### **Speech Recognition**

Implement Web Speech API with:

- Support for both `window.SpeechRecognition` and `window.webkitSpeechRecognition`
- Language: en-US
- `continuous: false` - stops after one phrase
- `interimResults: false` - only final results
- Error handling for microphone permission denials
- Console logging for debugging (log when started, recognized speech, and ended)
- Prevent duplicate entries by tracking processed transcripts

**Critical:** Ensure only ONE entry is created per voice input by:

1. Using a ref to track the last processed transcript
2. Only processing new transcripts that differ from the last one
3. Immediately resetting the transcript after processing

### **Food Parsing Logic**

When speech is recognized, parse the input using pattern matching for these foods (in order of priority):

1. Chicken breast / chicken: 165 cal, 31g protein, 0g carbs, 3.6g fat
2. Salmon: 206 cal, 22g protein, 0g carbs, 13g fat
3. Rice: 130 cal, 2.7g protein, 28g carbs, 0.3g fat
4. Broccoli: 55 cal, 3.7g protein, 11g carbs, 0.6g fat
5. Egg: 155 cal, 13g protein, 1.1g carbs, 11g fat
6. Banana: 105 cal, 1.3g protein, 27g carbs, 0.4g fat
7. Apple: 95 cal, 0.5g protein, 25g carbs, 0.3g fat
8. Oatmeal: 150 cal, 5g protein, 27g carbs, 3g fat
9. Protein shake: 120 cal, 24g protein, 3g carbs, 1.5g fat
10. Almonds: 160 cal, 6g protein, 6g carbs, 14g fat
11. Sweet potato: 112 cal, 2g protein, 26g carbs, 0.1g fat
12. Steak: 271 cal, 25g protein, 0g carbs, 19g fat
13. Turkey: 135 cal, 30g protein, 0g carbs, 0.7g fat
14. Tuna: 132 cal, 28g protein, 0g carbs, 1.3g fat
15. Pasta: 220 cal, 8g protein, 43g carbs, 1.3g fat
16. Bread: 265 cal, 9g protein, 49g carbs, 3.2g fat
17. Avocado: 160 cal, 2g protein, 8.5g carbs, 15g fat
18. Yogurt: 100 cal, 10g protein, 13g carbs, 0.4g fat
19. Milk: 103 cal, 8g protein, 12g carbs, 2.4g fat

Use case-insensitive regex pattern matching. If no match found, default to: 200 cal, 10g protein, 20g carbs, 8g fat.

### **Functionality**

**On Load:**

- Fetch all food entries from today (since midnight)
- Calculate and display total macros
- Order entries by created_at descending (newest first)

**When User Speaks:**

1. Show "Processing..." status
2. Parse the spoken text to identify food
3. Insert entry into database
4. Reload entries and recalculate totals
5. Show "Entry saved!" message for 2 seconds
6. Prevent multiple entries from same speech input

**Delete Entry:**

- Click trash icon to delete specific entry
- Reload and recalculate totals

**Reset Button:**

- Delete all entries from today
- Clear the display

### **Tech Stack**

- React with TypeScript
- Vite for build tooling
- Tailwind CSS for styling
- Bolt Database for database and backend
- Lucide React for icons (Mic, MicOff, Trash2, Menu, MessageSquare)
- Web Speech API for voice recognition

### **Component Structure**

`App.tsx (main component)
├── VoiceInput.tsx (microphone button with speech recognition)
├── MacroDisplay.tsx (2x2 grid of macro cards)
├── FoodEntryList.tsx (list of today's entries)
└── hooks/useSpeechRecognition.ts (custom hook for speech API)`

### **State Management**

App maintains:

- `entries` - array of today's food entries
- `totalMacros` - calculated totals for all macros
- `isProcessing` - prevents duplicate submissions
- `statusMessage` - user feedback

## **Important Implementation Details**

1. All timestamps should filter for "today" starting at midnight
2. Speech recognition must only process each transcript once
3. Error states must be clearly communicated to users
4. Microphone permission prompts should be explained
5. The app should handle speech recognition starting/stopping gracefully
6. Use proper TypeScript types for all data structures
7. Follow React best practices with proper hooks and state management

Build this as a production-ready, fully functional application with excellent user experience and error handling.</code></pre>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="example-section">
        <h2>Example: App Idea Prompt – SkillRally</h2>
        <p>This prompt describes a complete concept and design spec for a skills-based hiring platform called SkillRally where developers compete in technical challenges created by companies to win job offers and prizes. The app uses a dark-themed, premium UI with glassmorphism.</p>

        <div class="prompt-container">
          <div class="prompt-header">
            <span class="prompt-label">SkillRally App Idea Prompt</span>
          </div>
          <pre class="prompt-content"><code>Create a fully functional React application called "SkillRally" using Tailwind CSS, Lucide React icons, and React Router.

### App Overview

SkillRally is a skills-based hiring platform where developers compete in technical challenges (created by companies) to win job offers and prizes. The app features a dark-themed, premium UI with glassmorphism effects.

### Design System

- **Theme:** Dark mode only. Backgrounds: Zinc-900 (#09090b) and Zinc-800 (#18181b).
- **Primary Color:** Emerald (#22c55e) for buttons, accents, and progress bars.
- **Typography:** "Space Grotesk" for headings/display; "Inter" for body text.
- **Styling:** Gradients, subtle borders (zinc-700), and backdrop blur throughout.

### Pages &amp; Routing

**1. Navbar (Global)**

- Sticky top with glass effect (backdrop-blur).
- Logo: Trophy icon inside a rounded-xl container with emerald gradient background; text "SkillRally" with "Rally" having a gradient text effect.
- Links: Explore Contests, Create Contest (styled as CTA button).
- Mobile-responsive hamburger menu.

**2. Home Page (/)**

- **Hero:** Centered headline "Prove Your Skills. Get Hired." with background image overlay. Two CTAs: "Start Competing" and "Post a Challenge".
- **Stats Banner:** Horizontal strip with stats such as "$250k+ Prize Pool", "45 Active Challenges", "1.2k Developers".
- **Contest Grid:** Cards for each contest. Each card shows: company logo, role title, difficulty badge (Junior / Mid / Senior / Expert), tech stack tags, prize pool, and deadline. Hover: subtle glow or border highlight.

**3. Create Contest Page (/create)**

- Multi-step wizard for creating a hiring challenge with AI assistance.
- **Step 1:** Form fields for Company Name, Hiring Role, Difficulty (button group), and Topic.
- **Action:** "Generate with AI" button. Call an LLM API (OpenAI or similar) via a generic, swappable service layer (e.g., single module that accepts an API key and returns structured JSON). The API should return a JSON object with: title, description, deliverables (array), techStack (array), evaluationCriteria (array).
- **Step 2:** Show generated content in a preview card. "Publish Challenge" adds the contest to app state and redirects to home.

**4. Contest Detail Page (/contest/:id)**

- **Header:** Banner with company logo, contest title, countdown timer, and prominent "Submit Solution" button.
- **Tabs:** "Challenge Details" and "Leaderboard".
- **Details Tab:** Full description, deliverables list, tech stack tags, and resources.
- **Leaderboard Tab:** Table with Rank, Candidate Name/Avatar, Score (progress bar), Status (Hired, In Review, Submitted). Use mock data.
- **Submit Modal:** Form for GitHub URL and notes; submit adds entry to state or mock list.

### Data

- Pre-populate with realistic mock data for well-known tech companies (e.g., Netflix, Spotify, Stripe, Airbnb). Use distinct logos and plausible technical descriptions for each contest.

### Technical Requirements

- Use react-router-dom for all navigation.
- Fully responsive layout (mobile, tablet, desktop).
- AI generation must use a generic service layer (single abstraction that can be swapped or configured with an API key; no provider logic in UI components).</code></pre>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="example-section">
        <h2>Example: Elite Copywriter Prompt</h2>
        <p>This prompt structures an AI assistant as an expert direct response copywriter using the AIDA framework (Attention-Interest-Desire-Action) to craft high-converting copy through psychological triggers and emotional storytelling.</p>

        <div class="prompt-container">
          <div class="prompt-header">
            <span class="prompt-label">Elite Copywriter Prompt Template</span>
          </div>
          <pre class="prompt-content"><code>----------------------
ELITE COPYWRITER
----------------------

<context>
You are working with a business owner who needs to craft high-converting copy using the AIDA framework (Attention-Interest-Desire-Action). This proven copywriting structure guides prospects through a psychological journey from initial awareness to taking action. The copy must hook immediately, build genuine interest, create irresistible desire, and drive clear action—all while sounding natural and benefit-focused, not salesy.
</context>

<role>
Adopt the role of an expert Direct Response Copywriter- a burned-out ad agency creative director who discovered that emotion-driven psychology converts 10x better than feature lists, and now obsessively studies consumer behavior patterns at 3am while dissecting winning campaigns. Your primary objective is to craft persuasive AIDA-based copy that moves prospects from cold awareness to hot action through strategic psychological triggers.
</role>

<response_guidelines>
Follow the AIDA framework structure with these copywriting principles:

ATTENTION (Hook): Open with a pattern-interrupt that stops the scroll. Use curiosity gaps, bold claims with proof, controversial truths, or specific pain points. Keep it punchy—under 10 words when possible. NO generic questions like "Want to make money?" Instead: "Your landing page is killing 87% of your traffic."

INTEREST (Build): Expand the hook with specific, relatable details that make prospects think "this is about me." Use storytelling micro-moments, unexpected statistics, or empathy-driven statements. Bridge from problem to possibility without revealing the full solution yet.

DESIRE (Amplify): Paint the transformation picture. Show the emotional payoff, not just features. Use sensory language, social proof hints, and contrast (before vs after). Make them feel the outcome in their bones. This is where you agitate the pain AND show the promised land.

ACTION (Convert): Crystal clear CTA with zero friction. Tell them exactly what to do next and why now. Remove ambiguity. Use action verbs + benefit. "Start your 7-day trial" beats "Learn more."

WRITING RULES:
- Gunning Fog index 8 (8th grade readability)
- Write "you" not "our customers" (audience perspective)
- No unnecessary adjectives/adverbs—every word earns its place
- No concluding paragraphs unless specified
- Grammar dependency framework—vary sentence structure
- Don't assume context—be specific
- No complex words unless necessary for authority
</response_guidelines>

<task_criteria>
Your task is to generate AIDA copy that converts. Take a deep breath and work on this problem step-by-step:

1. ANALYZE the offer, audience, and desired action
2. IDENTIFY the core transformation (what changes for them)
3. SELECT the strongest attention hook based on audience awareness level
4. BUILD interest through specificity and relatability
5. AMPLIFY desire through emotional payoff and social proof
6. CRAFT a frictionless call-to-action with urgency

FOCUS ON:
- One clear message per copy piece
- Emotional triggers over logical features
- Specificity over vague benefits
- Natural flow—no robotic transitions
- Benefit-driven language throughout

AVOID:
- Generic hooks ("Are you tired of...")
- Feature dumping without benefits
- Weak CTAs ("Click here" / "Learn more")
- Corporate jargon or buzzwords
- Concluding paragraphs that kill momentum
- Over-explanation—trust the reader's intelligence
</task_criteria>

<information_about_me>
● PRODUCT/SERVICE: [INSERT YOUR PRODUCT OR SERVICE NAME AND WHAT IT DOES]
● TARGET AUDIENCE: [INSERT WHO THIS IS FOR - BE SPECIFIC: DEMOGRAPHICS, PAIN POINTS, AWARENESS LEVEL]
● DESIRED ACTION: [INSERT WHAT YOU WANT THEM TO DO - BUY, SIGN UP, BOOK CALL, ETC]
● KEY BENEFIT/TRANSFORMATION: [INSERT THE #1 OUTCOME THEY GET - THE EMOTIONAL PAYOFF]
● PLATFORM/FORMAT: [INSERT WHERE THIS COPY WILL LIVE - EMAIL, LANDING PAGE, AD, SOCIAL POST]
</information_about_me>

<response_format>
<aida_copy>
**[ATTENTION]**
[Punchy hook - 1-2 sentences max]

**[INTEREST]**
[Build the story - 2-3 sentences with specificity]

**[DESIRE]**
[Paint the transformation - 3-4 sentences with emotional payoff]

**[ACTION]**
[Clear CTA with benefit - 1-2 sentences]
</aida_copy>

<copy_breakdown>
**Why This Hook Works:** [1 sentence explanation]
**Interest Strategy:** [1 sentence explanation]
**Desire Trigger:** [1 sentence explanation]
**CTA Psychology:** [1 sentence explanation]
</copy_breakdown>
</response_format></code></pre>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="example-section">
        <h2>Example: ICE Score Prioritization Prompt</h2>
        <p>This prompt guides you through the ICE (Impact, Confidence, Ease) Framework developed by Sean Ellis for scientifically prioritizing ideas, features, and experiments. It's designed to help you stop drowning in ideas and focus on what truly matters.</p>

        <div class="prompt-container">
          <div class="prompt-header">
            <span class="prompt-label">ICE Score Prioritization Prompt</span>
          </div>
          <pre class="prompt-content"><code>Act as a Growth Hacking and Prioritization Expert, specifically trained in Sean Ellis's ICE Framework. My goal is to stop drowning in ideas and scientifically prioritize what I should work on next.

Please guide me through the following process step-by-step. Do not move to the next step until I have completed the current one.

STEP 1: THE OPTIONS

Ask me to list the specific ideas, features, ads, or experiments I am currently considering.

STEP 2: THE SCORING

Once I provide the list, go through my items one by one (or in a batch, if the list is short) and ask me to provide a score from 1-10 for the following three variables. Remind me of these definitions if I get stuck:

- Impact (1-10): If this works, how big will the benefit be? (10=Massive, 1=Negligible)
- Confidence (1-10): How sure am I that this will work? (10=I have proof/data, 1=Total guess)
- Ease (1-10): How easy is this to build/do? (10=Done today, 1=Months of work)

STEP 3: THE CALCULATION

Calculate the final ICE score for each item. (Sum the three numbers: Impact + Confidence + Ease).

STEP 4: THE VERDICT

Present the final data in a structured table sorted by the highest ICE score. Explicitly identify the #1 task I must execute immediately and advise me to ignore the others until the first one is complete.

Start the session by asking me for my list of ideas.</code></pre>
        </div>

        <div class="prompt-analysis">
          <h3>Why This Prompt Works</h3>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Step-by-Step Structure:</strong> Enforces a sequential workflow that prevents rushing through the prioritization process</li>
            <li><strong>Clear Role Definition:</strong> Establishes the AI as an expert in the ICE Framework, ensuring accurate guidance</li>
            <li><strong>Explicit Definitions:</strong> Provides concrete scoring criteria with clear anchors (10 vs 1) to reduce ambiguity</li>
            <li><strong>Guided Interaction:</strong> Instructs the AI to not proceed until each step is complete, maintaining focus and thoroughness</li>
            <li><strong>Actionable Output:</strong> Ends with a specific recommendation table and explicit next action, removing decision paralysis</li>
            <li><strong>Batch Flexibility:</strong> Allows scoring items individually or in groups based on list length, adapting to different use cases</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
